<http://example.org/sen0414/ch04/diagrams> a owl:Ontology ; rdfs:label "SEN0414 ch04 narrative diagrams - ABox"@en ; owl:versionInfo "1.0.0" ; dcterms:identifier "sen0414_ch04_diagrams_abox_v1_0_0" ;
    rdfs:comment "The chapter's authored diagrams as cme:NarrativeDiagram individuals: generated by sen0414_page_data_v2_6_0.py from sen0414_ch04_diagrams_v1_0_0.py, typed by the CME narrative-to-diagram mapping (a module of cme_standards_adoption_v1_3_0.ttl, CME 0.16.0)."@en ; owl:imports <http://example.org/cme/narrative-diagram> .

chx:Diagram_FunctionCallFlow a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "What happens when a function is called"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:Workflow ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:ActivityDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 4, the Definition, Parameters and Return value sections: Function call (execution jumps to the first line in the function and begins executing the code there), Parameter and argument (the argument 'Al' is assigned to the parameter name), Function body (runs on each call to the end or to a return statement), Return statement (leaves the call with the value of its expression as the return value), Implicit None (Python adds return None to any function without a return statement)" ; cme:elementCount 15 ;
    cme:accompanies "FunctionCall" ; cme:accompanies "ParameterAndArgument" ; cme:accompanies "FunctionBody" ; cme:accompanies "ReturnStatement" ; cme:accompanies "ImplicitNone" ; rdfs:comment "the explanations describe what Python does with a call in order - remember the calling line, assign the arguments to the parameters, execute the body, choose the return value, return to the calling line - with two choices on the way, whether an argument was left out and whether a return statement is reached: a workflow"@en .
chx:Diagram_NameResolutionFlow a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "How Python decides which variable a name refers to"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:Workflow ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:ActivityDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 4, the Namespaces and Scope rules sections: Name resolution (a name is resolved using the nearest enclosing scope, in the order local, enclosing, global, built-in), Scope identification (global statement, assignment in the function, use without assignment), Global statement (in this function, eggs refers to the global variable), UnboundLocalError (a local variable used before a value is bound), Built-in scope (print, len and int are found in the builtins namespace), NameError (raised when no scope contains the name)" ; cme:elementCount 25 ;
    cme:accompanies "NameResolution" ; cme:accompanies "LocalScope" ; cme:accompanies "EnclosingScope" ; cme:accompanies "GlobalScope" ; cme:accompanies "BuiltinScope" ; cme:accompanies "NameError" ; rdfs:comment "the explanations describe a search in a fixed order - the local scope, the enclosing scope, the global scope, the built-in scope - with a question at each step and an error when every step fails: a workflow"@en .
chx:Diagram_FrameLife a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "The life of a frame on the call stack"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:LifeCycle ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:StateDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 4, the Call stack section: Frame object (the record Python creates for a function call and places on the call stack; it stores the line number of the original call), Call stack order (Python remembers which line called the function so that execution can return there when it encounters a return statement), Recursion (each call has its own frame), Traceback (the lines of the calls the exception passed through), Local scope (destroyed when the call returns)" ; cme:elementCount 13 ;
    cme:accompanies "FrameObject" ; cme:accompanies "CallStackOrder" ; cme:accompanies "CallStack" ; rdfs:comment "the explanations describe states a call's record is in - not yet created, placed on the stack, executing, waiting for another call, removed on return or by an exception - and the events that move it between them: a life cycle"@en .
chx:Diagram_CallAndErrorInteraction a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "A call that returns, and a call that raises an error caught by except"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:Interaction ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:SequenceDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 4, the Exceptions section: Try and except (the code that could have an error is put in a try clause, and execution moves to the start of the except clause if an error happens), Error in a called function (errors that occur in function calls in a try block will also be caught), ZeroDivisionError (raised when the second argument of a division is zero), Traceback (printed when an exception is not handled)" ; cme:elementCount 12 ;
    cme:accompanies "TryExcept" ; cme:accompanies "ErrorInCall" ; cme:accompanies "ZeroDivisionError" ; cme:accompanies "Traceback" ; rdfs:comment "the passage is an exchange of messages between parties - the code in the try clause, the function it calls and the except clause - in an order that matters, once with a return value and once with an error: an interaction"@en .
chx:Diagram_FunctionUses a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "What a programmer does with functions, and what Python does with them"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:SetOfUses ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:UseCaseDiagram ; cme:readFrom "chapter 4, the Definition, Return value, Annotations and Style sections: Deduplication (getting rid of copied-and-pasted code; a fix in one place), Function as a black box (its inputs are the parameters and its output is the return value), Call as expression (a call wherever a value is expected), Function annotation (a note on a parameter or the result, stored but not enforced), Function naming (lowercase with underscores), Comment (a note for the human reader that Python ignores)" ; cme:elementCount 13 ;
    cme:accompanies "Deduplication" ; cme:accompanies "BlackBoxFunction" ; cme:accompanies "CallAsExpression" ; cme:accompanies "TypeAnnotation" ; cme:accompanies "FunctionNaming" ; cme:accompanies "Comment" ; rdfs:comment "the passages list what a programmer does with functions - group repeated code, use a function as a black box, pass a call as an argument, annotate, name and comment it - and what Python does in return, which is a set of uses by actors, not a sequence"@en .
chx:Diagram_ParameterKindsMap a cme:NarrativeDiagram ; rdfs:label "The kinds of parameter and how a call fills them"@en ; cme:hasPattern cme:TopicAndParts ; cme:hasDiagramType cme:MindMap ; cme:readFrom "chapter 4, the Parameters section: Parameter and argument (the argument 'Al' is assigned to the parameter name), Positional argument (matched by its place in the call), Named parameter (identified by the name written before it, as in sep=','), Positional-only parameter (before a slash), Keyword-only parameter (after a star), Default argument (used when a call leaves the argument out; evaluated once when the function is defined)" ; cme:elementCount 21 ;
    cme:accompanies "Parameters" ; cme:accompanies "ParameterAndArgument" ; cme:accompanies "PositionalArgument" ; cme:accompanies "NamedParameter" ; cme:accompanies "PositionalOnlyParameter" ; cme:accompanies "KeywordOnlyParameter" ; cme:accompanies "DefaultArgument" ; rdfs:comment "the section classifies one topic, the parameter, by the way a call supplies its value - by position, by name, only by position, only by name, or not at all - and lists the rule under each: a topic and its parts, laid out to remember"@en .
